'''Tilgate stone.''' [So called from ''Tilgate'' Forest in Sussex, England.] In ''geol.'', the name given to beds of calcareous sandstone or ironstone occurring near Hastings, England, in the Ashdown sand, a subdivision of the Hastings beds, by which term the lower section of the Wealden series is known to English geologists. {{smaller|The name ''Tilgate stone'' was also given by Mantell to certain beds of calcareous sandstone occurring in the Wadhurst clay—also a local subdivision of the Ashdown sand, and so named from the village of Wadhurst, near Tunbridge Wells. This Tilgate stone is noted for its reptilian remains, becoming in places a regular bone-bed. See ''Wealden''.}}

{{smaller block|As pointed out by Mr. Topley, the "''Tilgate Stone''" of Dr. Mantell occurs at different horizons in different localities.
{{right|''Woodward'', Geol. of Eng. and Wales (2d ed.), p. 360.}}}}

